.TH INSTALLERD 8 2026-10-19 "Peios installer 0.1.13"
.SH NAME
installerd \- Peios installation and repair service
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B installerd
.RB [ \-\-socket
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-medium
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-force ]
.br
.B installerd \-\-dry-run
.RB [ \-\-socket
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-inventory
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-fail-at
.IR PHASE ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B installerd
owns installation, repair, and upgrade operations.
It serves a Message Surface Interaction Protocol (MSIP) conversation to
terminal, browser, and scripted frontends over a Unix-domain socket.
The daemon performs disk operations; the frontend presents questions and
forwards answers.
.PP
A fresh installation replaces the selected whole disk, copies the medium's
pristine system, stages installed-system registry seeds, and regenerates the
initramfs and unified kernel image.
Upgrade uses the same edition's newer release from the medium.
Repair offers boot-file regeneration, a filesystem check, or replacement of
the root directory's security descriptor.
The browser frontend supports fresh installation; use
.BR install-tui (1)
for upgrade and repair.
.PP
Installation attempts to copy the medium's GXWI key and certificate from
.IR /var/state/gxwi .
A missing key or failed copy does not fail installation.
Verify the certificate presented by the installed machine before entering
credentials; successful installation does not prove that it is unchanged.
Upgrade preserves the installed system's key.
.PP
The console's initial page reports the browser address and certificate
fingerprint when available and refreshes them every 3 seconds.
The fingerprint comes from
.IR /var/state/gxwi/certificate.sha256 .
Use a trusted console to check it; a page delivered over an unverified
connection cannot authenticate that connection.
.PP
Closing a frontend does not cancel an active job.
A daemon restart loses its in-memory conversation.
A completed job and an accepted restart request do not prove that the
machine booted from the selected disk.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-socket " PATH"
Listen on PATH instead of
.IR /run/installerd.sock .
.TP
.BI \-\-medium " PATH"
Use PATH as the mounted installation medium instead of
.IR /media/peios .
.TP
.B \-\-force
Allow the partitioning tool to replace a table it did not create. This never
suppresses the mounted-device guard or the confirmation in the MSIP flow.
.TP
.B \-\-dry-run
Exercise the complete conversation without changing disks. The machine's
disks and their partitions are listed as they are; what is on them is not
looked into, which takes a mount.
.TP
.BI \-\-inventory " FILE"
With
.BR \-\-dry-run ,
pretend to be the machine the JSON in FILE describes instead of probing this
one: its
.I disks
and what each holds, the storage
.I controllers
nothing drives, and the release its
.I medium
carries. For showing a surface a machine other than the one it is being
worked on. A key that is not recognized is an error.
.TP
.BI \-\-fail-at " PHASE"
With
.BR \-\-dry-run ,
fail every job part way through PHASE, which is named as the end of the
phase's element ref:
.BR partition ,
.BR format ,
.B copy
or
.B boot
for an installation. For showing a surface a job that goes wrong.
.TP
.BR \-h , " \-\-help"
Show command help.
.TP
.BR \-V , " \-\-version"
Show the program version.
.SH SECURITY
The service is intended to run as SYSTEM.
Its intended socket descriptor grants SYSTEM and Administrators access.
Descriptor application is best effort: failure of
.B sd
produces a warning and leaves inherited access in effect.
Verify runtime socket protection rather than inferring it from a listening
message.
.PP
Startup removes an existing entry at the socket path before binding,
including in dry-run mode.
Use a fresh, protected directory and an explicit socket path for rehearsals.
Dry-run avoids disk changes but is not free of startup effects.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Help or version was printed successfully.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid arguments or an unreadable or invalid inventory.
.TP
.B 101
An unrecoverable listener or server error caused a Rust panic.
The daemon normally continues serving rather than exiting when a job ends.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PATH
Used to locate external installation, filesystem, registry, and security
tools.
Run the privileged daemon with a trusted executable search path.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /run/installerd.sock
Default MSIP socket.
.TP
.I /media/peios/rootfs.squashfs
Default pristine system image.
.TP
.I /var/state/gxwi
GXWI's key and certificate, carried into an installed system; see
.BR gxwid (1).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gxwid (1),
.BR install-tui (1),
.BR msip-drive (1),
.BR peipkg (8)
